  "audio": "029.delegation-also-unlocks.mp3",
  "paragraph": 29,
  "text": "Delegation also unlocks real parallelism when tasks are genuinely independent. An orchestrator can fan out three specialist workers across separate git worktrees, one updating backend tests, one adjusting UI state, and one updating docs, and have their branches converge cleanly at a shared verification gate. Just watch out for the classic multi-agent trap: never delegate tightly coupled sequential steps across subagents. When two agents edit overlapping logic, merge conflicts and coordination overhead quickly perform worse than one focused single-agent loop.",
